message.content:
Below is a minimal “recipe” that shows **how** to pull a *reactive* queue called `taskQueue`
from Redisson while telling it to serialize its elements with your own codec (`jsonCodec`).  
Everything runs on the Reactor / RxJava style APIs – no callbacks, only `Mono`, `Flux`, …  

---

### 1️⃣ Add the right dependencies

```xml
<!-- Maven -->
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.redisson</groupId>
    <artifactId>redisson-reactive</artifactId>
    <version>${redisson.version}</version>
</dependency>

<!-- If you’re not yet using Jackson’s JSON codec from Redisson itself -->
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.redisson</groupId>
    <artifactId>redisson-codec-jackson-json</artifactId>
    <version>${redisson.version}</version>
</dependency>
```

*(If you wrote your own codec implementation, replace the second line with whatever JAR contains it.)*

---

### 2️⃣ Create a configuration that tells Redisson to use your codec

```java
import org.redisson.config.Config;
import org.redisson.codec.Codec;

// Replace this with however you instantiate your codec.
// Example: a simple wrapper around Jackson's ObjectMapper:
class MyJsonCodec implements Codec {
    private static final ObjectMapper MAPPER = new ObjectMapper();

    @Override public byte[] encode(Object obj) throws IOException { /* ... */ }

    @Override public <T> T decode(byte[] data, Class<T> clazz) throws IOException { /* ... */ }
}
```

Now wire it into the config:

```java
Config cfg = new Config();
cfg.setCodec(new MyJsonCodec());          // <-- global default codec
// OR set per‑resource codec later when fetching the queue
```

(If you prefer to keep the global codec generic and override only for one resource, skip setting it here.)

---

### 3️⃣ Build the **reactive** client

```java
import org.redisson.api.RedissonReactiveClient;
import org.redisson.Redisson;

RedissonReactiveClient redisson =
        Redisson.createReactive(cfg);   // ← note the *createReactive*
```

---

### 4️⃣ Grab the queue reactively

#### Option A – Using the globally configured codec

```java
import org.redisson.api.RDeque;

RDeque<MyTaskType> taskQueue = 
        redisson.getReactiveDeque("taskQueue");     // returns a Flux/Mono based deque
```

All subsequent calls such as `.add()`, `.poll()` return `Mono<Void>` / `Mono<Task>` respectively.

#### Option B – Override the codec for this specific queue

If you didn’t set the codec globally, supply it explicitly:

```java
import org.redisson.api.RDeque;

MyJsonCodec myCodec = new MyJsonCodec();

RDeque<MyTaskType> taskQueue = 
        redisson.<MyTaskType>getReactiveDeque(
                "taskQueue",
                myCodec            // <- explicit codec for this queue
        );
```

---

### 5️⃣ Consume / Produce items

```java
// Producer side
Mono<Void> pushJob = taskQueue.add(myNewTask);

// Consumer side
Flux<MyTaskType> streamOfJobs = taskQueue.poll()
                                        .repeatWhenEmpty(Duration.ofMillis(500))
                                        .takeUntilOther(someStopSignal)
                                        .doOnNext(task -> process(task));
```

Because everything is reactive, you can compose, back‑pressure, retry, cancel… all within the normal Reactor/Rx chain.

---

## TL;DR Code Snippet

```java
// 1. Configure & build client
Config cfg = new Config().setCodec(new MyJsonCodec());
RedissonReactiveClient rclient = Redisson.createReactive(cfg);

// 2. Get the reactive queue
RDeque<MyTaskDto> taskQueue = rclient.getReactiveDeque("taskQueue");

// 3. Work with it reactively
taskQueue.add(new MyTaskDto(...))           // Mono<Void>
         .thenMany(taskQueue.take())       // Flux<MyTaskDto>
         .subscribe(System.out::println);
```

That’s all! You now have a fully reactive queue backed by Redisson, serializing/deserializing via your chosen `jsonCodec`.

